WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:01:08.000
We've all heard the advice. If you want to secure financial future, save for retirement, and start early, track your spending, don't spend more than you earn. But rules like these are far easier said than followed, especially if you're short on time, money, or both. Knowing this, some behavioral scientists are interested in learning more about how people make financial decisions and then using what they learn to help improve financial well-being, especially for people with low to moderate incomes. So why is it so hard to take the financial actions that we know we ought to be taking? What gets in the way? How do financial stress and anxiety affect us and our relationships? And how am I talking more openly about our finances help? What actions can you take now to set yourself on the best financial path? And what research-based actions could employers and institutions take to improve financial well-being for everyone? Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that examines the links between psychological science and everyday life.

2
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:54.000
I'm Kim Mills. My guest today is Dr. Wendy DeLerosa, an assistant professor at the Wharton School at the University of Pennsylvania. She focuses on using behavioral science to improve consumers' financial well-being. Her award-winning research has been published in many scientific journals and featured in the New York Times. The Wall Street Journal, CNN, and NPR, among other places. Dr. De La Rosa is also the co-creator and host of Ted's Your Money and Your Mind series, the co-founder of the Common Sense Lab, and a Forbes 30 under 30 financial honoree. Before joining Wharton, she helped start Google's first behavioral economics unit,

3
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:36.000
and she began her career as a private equity investor at Goldman Sachs. Dr. DeLarosa, thank you for joining me. Thank you for having me. It's a pleasure to be here. So I just mentioned that we all know these seemingly simple financial rules, but that knowing them doesn't mean that it's easy to put them into practice. Why is that? What gets in the way of people making the best financial steps for themselves? Oh, Kim, that's a million-dollar question. I think we kind of inherently know that answer because life is hard, because this is hard. one of the things that I tend to tell people is that we infantilize financial behaviors.

4
00:02:36.000 --> 00:03:29.000
You know, none of us think about saving the world from climate change by saying, let's get everybody in a room and teach them how to recycle. And that's how we're going to save the world. But for financial behaviors, for whatever reason, because especially in Western societies, we think about financial success. as a purely individualistic pursuit, we think, hey, let's get people in a room, teach them how to budget, and that will surely fix any financial anxieties. And so what we know from existing research is that that's just not the most effective tool. Because fundamentally, we all know what we need to do. The equation is not that hard. We all have subscriptions that we know we should cancel. we'll know that we should increase our retirement savings, right?

5
00:03:29.000 --> 00:04:21.000
The hard part is that right now is never the right time because right now I have to answer my work email because right now I have to go pick up my kids because right now I just don't have the time and also because I feel a level of shame around not being where I want to be. That's interesting that you talk about shame because a lot of financial advice columns and books have titles like top 10 financial mistakes to avoid. And I know you've said you don't like to frame people's financial behaviors as mistakes or bad decisions. Why do you feel that way? Well, I think it's because we have to be really honest about the world that we're in. Almost every company is getting smarter, more efficient, faster at getting you to part with your mind.

6
00:04:21.000 --> 00:05:20.000
money. It's getting easier to spend not harder, right? The donut is getting tastier, not less tasty. And so we also have to recognize that our brains are not necessarily set up to think about the world and fundamentally understand compound interests. And so you have this world where you have a David versus not just one Goliath, but a David versus thousands of Goliath. And so what I want people to take away is that if you're struggling with your financial situation, you are not the only one. And it's not because you have this like personal failing. It's because it's actually really hard to do. Costs are rising. Wages are barely keeping up. And managing this complex world of finances where, you know, the average person in New York City sees thousands of

7
00:05:20.000 --> 00:06:03.000
ads and thousands of opportunities to spend money is just really difficult. And once we do this thing where we tend to internalize shame, that leads to more negative behavior because that leads us to ignore our problems, to not talk about our problems, to not want to find solutions to our financial well-being. And so I want to get rid of the shame that we feel about the fact that we're losing a battle against thousands of Goliaths. What about the role of financial education in helping to improve people's financial well-being? Are the programs that are taught in schools and workplaces helpful at all? Is there a way to do them better?

8
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:46.000
That's a great question. So I'll tell you from a study I conducted where I asked roughly about 1,000 households, what are some things that you could do in the next month to improve your financial security? over 94% of the households could list three or more things that they could do in the next month to improve their financial security. So fundamentally people know what they need to do. Like we talked about, it's just hard. And the research on the efficacy of financial education on financial behaviors is mixed at best, right? Why is that, well, you know, you can learn how to budget when you're in school, but ultimately your environment.

9
00:06:46.000 --> 00:07:22.000
is such a bigger predictor about how you spend your money than whatever class you took, you know, X many years ago. That's not to say that financial education doesn't play a role, but we have to recognize that it takes more than just financial education. It's about changing your environment and setting yourself up for success. What about people's basic mindsets? For example, I know you've done research looking at earning versus, is saving, and you found that people seem to be predisposed to favor earning more money as

10
