that sort of relinquishes or abdicates the financial management responsibilities. And in a study by Adrian Ward and John Lynch, what they found is that this is actually really problematic. it because as couples go through their life, that partner that takes on all their financial management skills, they become more and more adept over time. But that partner that has relinquished their control, it's not that they stay the same. They become less and less adept at managing their finances over time. And why is this problematic? Well, God forbid you break up or you get divorce. Now that partner that has relinquished all of that responsibility is in a worse financial situation because they have less financial management skills than they had when they started the

15
00:11:43.000 --> 00:12:26.000
relationship. And a lot of the time, sadly, that ends up being in heteronormative couples, that ends up being the women. And of course, we've all heard the stories about the couple gets divorced and the woman discovers that her ex has drained the bank accounts, that there's no money left because she paid no attention, right? She left it all up to him. So I can see how that's a big problem in a lot of relationships. But, you know, the other thing is that we've learned that talking about money is impolite, right? And you're a big proponent of talking openly and honestly about money, not just with your partners, but with your friends and family. So why is it important to have those conversations? What should we be talking about that we're not?

16
00:12:26.000 --> 00:13:10.000
Well, it's so important because we know from research that you can get so many better. benefits from talking about your finances with other people. For example, when we think about our relationship with our friends, we're much more likely to know our friends' dating history and all the baggage that comes with that than about their retirement accounts or whether or not they are in debt. And when we think about our relationships with our friends and what we know about their financial situation, we tend to know every time they spend and or get into debt. We know when they buy a new car or they buy a new watch. We know effectively when they go on a large or lavish trip.

17
00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:36.000
But we don't really know whether or not they're saving for retirement. For whatever reason, that becomes taboo. And if we're friends, if we're genuinely friends, I care about your well-being and you care about my well-being. Why not talk about one of the most important parts and predictors of our general well-being, which is our financial security? I might learn something from you, you might learn something from me. And this is not hypothetical.

18
00:13:36.000 --> 00:14:30.000
Our friends can also hold us accountable. One of the lovely studies that I love shows that when friends come together and share their savings goals out loud and hold each other accountable week after week, people end up saving more. So our friends are here to just help us in all sorts of domains, including our financial domain. The other piece I would say is you don't know what you don't know, especially if you don't talk about money. How will you ever know if you're underpaid if you don't ever ask other people what they make? Now, that's something, though, that tends to be highly discouraged in workplaces, people sharing with each other who makes how much money. I mean, how do we overcome that when your employer is telling you, don't talk about this. Don't tell the person in the next cubicle how much money you're making because maybe she's making 10,000.

19
00:14:30.000 --> 00:15:29.000
in a year more than you are and doing the same job? What will you do when you find that out? Well, I think we have to think about who does that help? Silence and lack of transparency tends to help the employer because they can think about different ways in which to pay people differently, especially people who are doing the same job, right? And so once you recognize, like, who is the silence actually helping, then you can empower yourself and say, well, it's not helping me. Right? It's not helping me. And, you know, people think about wages as sort of a zero-sum game. A lot of the times, right, if I learn that I'm getting underpaid, I can now gather the information and come to my employer with a better sense of skills of saying, hey, this is the skill so that I bring

20
00:15:29.000 --> 00:16:08.000
to the table and this is what I know that I'm worth. But let's say it's the other way around where I'm the person that's quote unquote overpaid compared to my colleague. It doesn't hurt me at all if my colleague increases their income. In fact, it now creates an opportunity for me to make more money right now that the baseline has reset. And so I want people to think about this as it's never really a zero. sum game and thinking about finances as a zero-sum game can sometimes lead to really problematic

21
00:16:08.000 --> 00:17:08.000
competitive behaviors in a way that it doesn't have to be. I want to ask about your family background. Was money discussed openly when you were a kid? How did you get interested in studying how people relate to and manage their money? I ran away from this topic for so long. long. This is the last thing I thought I was going to be doing. So I'll give you a little bit of my background. I was born in the Dominican Republic. My family immigrated from the Dominican Republic to the Bronx, like many families in hopes of just a better financial life. And the lack of money, I like to joke and say, was the sixth person at the dinner table. It was constantly a topic of conversation. We were very much aware in my family that we did not have a lot of resources.

22
00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:53.000
And so I took it upon myself at that time to think about, well, how can I try to earn as much as possible in a short time period as possible? And I sort of shied away from my history. I didn't want to tell people that I came from little memes. I pretended for a long time to be somebody that I wasn't. And it wasn't until I realized how much of a burden and weight and shame, how much negativity that was sort of feeding into my life, how exhausting it was to put on this mask every day, that I started to flourish as a person.

23
00:17:53.000 --> 00:18:24.000
Because then I could be authentic. and say, hey, actually, here are all the concerns that I have. How have you dealt with this? And now all of a sudden, people could connect with me in a different way. They gave me tips. They gave me more opportunities because they knew the path that I was on. And it was, again, by talking about money and being open and frank about my history of, you know, financial insecurity, that it led people to become much more interested in me and give me more opportunity.

24
00:18:24.000 --> 00:19:14.000
But I will tell you, because I spent so many years running away from that history, this is the last thing that I thought I would be doing. And now it's the only thing that I wish to be doing. I'm living my dream life. I'm doing things that I are helping people like my family. Speaking of which, I know you've been interested in how money issues affect kids, particularly the children of immigrants, when they have to step into that parental. role and help with family finances. What do you find when you look at that situation? So the first thing we have to recognize is that poverty is trauma. And a lot of times we desensitize children who grew up in poverty. We think that they're more resilient. We think that they're

